course: everyday
lesson: 329
title: Breakfast, the register, the timetable, corridors, earthquake drills, pick-up, phones, tidy desks, homework and group work
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A school day goes well when you are ready for it: eat something simple for breakfast, even if you are in a hurry, and check your timetable the night before.
explain: At school, answer «here» or «present» clearly when the register or roll call is taken, walk calmly on the correct side of the corridor, and keep your desk tidy.
explain: In an earthquake drill you drop, take cover under a desk and hold on until the shaking stops, because falling objects cause most injuries.
explain: Example: a boy in Seattle waits after school, but his usual adult does not come to pick him up. He goes back into the school office and tells a teacher, who phones his family; he does not walk off or get into a car he was not told about.
explain: Homework must be in your own words: copying text from the internet and passing it off as yours is plagiarism, a kind of cheating; say where you found the facts.
fact: Eat at least something simple such as toast, fruit or cereal before school; the brain needs food to concentrate, and you may feel tired or grumpy by mid-morning without it.
fact: When the register or roll call is taken, answer clearly «here» or «present», because the school must know which children are in the building in case of an emergency.
fact: Learn your school timetable and check it the night before, so you bring the right things and know where to be.
fact: In the corridor, walk on the correct side, do not run and keep your voice low, because corridors are crowded and running causes collisions and falls.
fact: In an earthquake drill: drop, cover under a sturdy desk and hold on until the shaking stops.
fact: If your usual adult does not come to pick you up, go back into the school office and tell a teacher; do not walk off or accept a lift from someone you were not told about.
fact: Follow your school's rule about mobile phones, usually keeping the phone switched off in your bag or handing it in.
fact: At the end of a lesson, put books, pencils and papers back where they belong, so you find things quickly and the room stays safe.
fact: Copying homework from the internet and passing it off as yours is plagiarism; write in your own words and say where you found the facts.
fact: When a group disagrees about a task, listen to each idea, then vote or try a mix of ideas.
exercise: You are late and want to skip breakfast before school. What is best? | Eat something simple like toast or fruit | Skip it and eat double at lunch | Eat only sweets on the way | Drink nothing and eat nothing until home time
exercise: Why should you not skip breakfast before school? | The brain needs food to concentrate | Breakfast makes you grow taller each day | Teachers check what you ate at the gate | You are not allowed in without it, by law
exercise: The teacher is taking the register (roll call). What do you do when your name is called? | Answer clearly «here» or «present» | Stay quiet, the teacher can see you | Answer for your absent friend as well | Wave a hand without looking up from your book
exercise: Why does the school take the register? | To know exactly which children are in the building in an emergency | To see who has the neatest uniform and give out uniform points each week | To choose who sits at the front | To count how many lunches are left
exercise: When is a good time to check your school timetable? | The night before | Only when you arrive at school | Once at the start of the year | When the lesson is over
exercise: Why learn your school timetable or schedule? | To bring the right things and know where to be | To know exactly when the teachers have their lunch break | To skip the lessons you do not like | To know when the holidays start
exercise: How should you move between classrooms in a busy corridor? | Walk on the correct side, not run, keep your voice low | Run fast down the middle so you are never late for a lesson | Walk in the middle and chat loudly | Push past slower children
exercise: Why is running in school corridors a bad idea? | Corridors are crowded and running causes collisions | Running makes the floor dirty and the cleaners get cross | Teachers cannot hear the bell over the footsteps | It wears out your shoes
exercise: During an earthquake drill at school in New Zealand, what do you do? | Drop, cover under a desk and hold on | Run out of the building at once, before anything falls | Stand by the window to watch the ground move | Hide in a cupboard full of heavy shelves
exercise: Why take cover under a sturdy desk in an earthquake? | Falling objects cause most injuries and the desk protects your head | Desks are fixed to the floor, so they stop the ground from shaking under you | The floor is the warmest place | Teachers can count you more easily
exercise: After school, your usual adult does not come to pick you up. What do you do? | Go back to the school office and tell a teacher | Start walking home on your own the way you usually go | Accept a lift from a friendly stranger | Wait alone at the gate until dark
exercise: A neighbour you were not told about offers you a lift home when your parent is late. What is right? | Say no and go back to tell a teacher | Say yes, because you know her face from your street | Say yes if she knows your name | Say yes if it is raining
exercise: You bring a mobile phone to school. What should you do? | Follow the school rule, usually switched off in your bag or handed in | Keep it on your desk so you can check messages between your lessons | Use it in lessons if you are quiet | Play games with it during class
exercise: The lesson ends and your desk is covered in books and pencils. What do you do? | Put things back where they belong | Leave it all for the cleaner to sort out | Push everything into a pile on the floor | Put it all in a friend's tray instead
exercise: For homework you find a great paragraph on a website. What is honest? | Write it in your own words and say where you found the facts | Copy it word for word, because a website is free for anyone to use | Copy it but change one word | Copy it and say you wrote it yourself
exercise: What is plagiarism? | Copying someone's work and passing it off as yours | Writing facts in your own words and saying where you found them | Asking a teacher for help | Reading many books for one project
exercise: Your group disagrees about how to build a model. What is fair? | Listen to each idea, then vote or mix ideas | Let the loudest person decide | Each person builds alone and stops talking | Give up and tell the teacher it is impossible
test: A girl skips breakfast every day. By mid-morning she cannot think in maths. What would help most? | A quick breakfast such as cereal or a banana | Going to bed later so she gets more rest in the evening | Sitting nearer the window | Drinking a fizzy drink at break
test: A fire alarm goes off. Why is it important that the register was taken that morning? | Teachers can check that every child is out | It decides which class leaves the building first and which goes last | It helps the fire engine find the school | It tells parents what time to come
test: A boy forgets his PE kit every Tuesday. What would solve this? | Checking the timetable the night before | Bringing PE kit only on Fridays | Asking the teacher to cancel PE | Wearing PE kit to every lesson
test: The ground starts shaking during a lesson in Los Angeles. Which is right? | Get under your desk, hold on and stay until it stops | Run to the door and down the stairs before the building moves more | Stand up on your chair | Go to the window to see outside
test: A girl's grandma is very late for pick-up. A man she does not know says, «Your grandma sent me». What should she do? | Go back into the school office and tell a teacher | Go with him, because he knows about grandma and sounds friendly | Walk home alone instead | Wait outside the gate with him
test: Which is the usual rule for phones in many schools? | Switched off in your bag or handed in, used only for emergencies | Always switched on, so that you can answer messages during lessons | Allowed only for games at lunch | Phones must be left at home by law
test: Why is a tidy table good for the whole class? | You find things quickly and the room is safe | It makes lessons shorter for everyone | It gets you a reward every single day | It stops the bell from ringing
test: Two children in a group each want their own poster design. What is a fair way to decide? | Hear both ideas, then vote or combine them | The one who shouts loudest wins | The taller child decides | Nobody makes the poster
test: A boy prints an article and hands it in as his own story. What has he done? | Plagiarism, a kind of cheating | Good research for a project | Teamwork with the author | A fair summary of the article
explain: A boy in New Zealand ran down the corridor looking at his phone and crashed into a classmate carrying a tray; both fell and one cut his chin.
explain: When nobody came to pick up a girl in Texas, she walked home alone; her mum arrived late and panicked, and the police were called before the girl was found safe at home.
fact: Running in corridors causes falls and bumps into other children.
fact: A child who leaves school without telling anyone can start a frightening search.
test: mum's late, i'll just walk home | Go back to the office and tell a teacher | Walk home alone, it's not that far really | Wait at the bus stop | Go with a friend's older brother
test: running in the corridor cos im late | Walk; you could crash into someone | Run faster so it's quicker | Run if nobody sees | Run on the stairs
test: earthquake drill is so boring, why do it | So you know to drop, cover and hold on in a real one | It's only for teachers, kids just sit there and wait it out | Real quakes don't happen | It's to miss a lesson
test: did u hear a kid left school without telling anyone and "the whole town was out looking, helicopters and all". lesson? | Never leave school without telling anyone; it starts a frightening search | It's fine if you go straight home and text your mum once you get there | Nobody notices when kids leave | Teachers don't care where you are
test: my mate says two kids ran round the corner in the corridor and "both got concussion". what's the true bit | Running in corridors causes falls and bumps | Corridors are made for running | Only teachers can get hurt | Corners are always safe
test: kids say the girl who copied her homework off the internet "is a cheater and her parents do all her work". pass it on? | No, don't spread it; write in your own words yourself | Yes, she's a cheat | Yes, tell the teacher about her parents | Yes, everyone does it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the earthquake drill? | Drop, take cover under a sturdy desk and hold on until the shaking stops | Leave the building and run outdoors at the very first tremor that is felt | Stand beside the windows | Remain seated without cover
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a pupil answers the roll call clearly. | The school must know who is in the building in an emergency | It improves the class attendance records so that prizes can be awarded | The teacher marks pupils by their voices | It is required for lunch orders
exam: It is given that a pupil's usual adult does not arrive at pick-up. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | The pupil returns to the school office and tells a teacher | The pupil accepts a lift from another parent who has not been asked | The pupil walks home alone | The pupil waits outside the gate until dark
exam: State the term for copying text from the internet and passing it off as one's own. | Plagiarism | Referencing | Summarising | A quotation
